"""
`FurutaMPC` split across two clocks: the encoders are read and the state estimated on a fast
clock, the MPC solves on a slow one.

The point is that the velocity estimate no longer has to trade lag against the MPC's period. At
10 ms the single-rate controller has to run its estimator nearly unfiltered (`velocity_filter`
0.8, a 6.2 ms time constant) because more lag destabilizes the balancing; estimating five times
per MPC tick removes that constraint.

What replaces the estimator matters more than the extra rate. `VelocityEstimator` is a backward
difference through an exponential filter -- a constant-velocity estimator -- and on a swinging
pendulum the velocity is never locally constant, so its estimate carries a bias proportional to
the acceleration that no bandwidth choice removes. Sampling the same estimator faster is
therefore worth almost nothing: measured against the truth on an angle quantized to encoder
counts, it goes from 0.141 to 0.164 rad/s RMS while balancing. A constant-*acceleration*
estimator removes the bias: `DiscreteComponents.AlphaBetaGammaFilter` at 1 ms scores 0.035 rad/s
while balancing and 0.243 during the swing-up, against 0.141 and 1.280 for the 10 ms baseline --
four and five times better at once. See NOTES.md for the comparison and its method.

The tracker's own position estimate feeds the MPC, not the raw encoder angle, so the state the
solver sees is one filter's coherent (position, velocity) pair rather than a raw angle beside a
filtered rate. Feeding the raw angle instead is a rewire of `latest_shoulder_angle.u`.

The transition to the slow clock is four `DiscreteComponents.Latest` blocks, one per MPC state
input. Each keeps its input on the fast clock and puts its output on the MPC's, so the MPC reads
the most recent value the fast partition produced. The slow period is an integer multiple of the
fast one whose tick instants coincide exactly in floating point -- at the defaults every multiple
of `Ts` is a multiple of `Ts_fast` to the last bit -- so the MPC reads a value produced at that
same instant. The transition therefore adds no delay.

The pendulum angle is wrapped *after* it is sub-sampled. `mod` is memoryless so the value is the
same either way, and this order leaves every `Latest` source a variable of the fast partition
rather than an algebraic function of one.

Neither clock is declared here. The enclosing model folds one `PeriodicClock` into the
measurement net and another into the command net, the way `FurutaMPCSwingup` declares its single
clock; the fast clock arrives through `shoulder_angle`/`elbow_angle` and the slow one through `u`.
Both are load-bearing -- without the slow one nothing pins the MPC's partition and the `Latest`
reads have no reading clock. `Ts` must equal the slow clock's period, since it is also the MPC's
shooting interval.

The horizon is laid out on a *non-uniform* shooting grid. `MPCComponents.linear_time_steps`
divides the `horizon` into `Np` intervals that grow by a constant increment from `Ts`, so the
horizon costs `Np` decision variables instead of the `horizon/Ts` a uniform grid of the same
resolution would need -- 50 against 160 at the defaults. The first interval stays at `Ts`, since
its control is the one that is applied and held for one clock period, and the stage cost of each
interval is weighted by its length over `Ts`, so a long interval counts for as much as the short
ones it replaces.

The grid's cost is accuracy far out: acados integrates every interval with one step of the same
`integrator_stages`-stage explicit Runge-Kutta scheme, and the last interval is
`2*horizon/Np - Ts`, 5.4 times the first at the defaults. Measured against a finely integrated
reference over the states a swing-up traverses, one 27 ms step of the 2-stage scheme is off by up
to 4 rad/s against 0.03 for one 5 ms step. It does not matter in closed loop:
`integrator_stages = 4` costs 65 % more solve time (1.36 ms at the median against 0.82) and swings
up no better. See NOTES.md, which also records that the 0.8 s horizon is *not* what the rate
increase buys -- a uniform 0.25 s grid at the same `Ts` and `Np` (`horizon = Np * Ts`) catches
sooner and leaves the solver healthier.

Everything else -- the energy-shaping stage cost, the soft state bounds, `reset_on_failure`, the
terminal LQR cost -- is `FurutaMPC`'s and is documented there.
"""
component FurutaMPCMultirate
  "Measured arm (shoulder) angle, on the fast clock"
  shoulder_angle = Dyad.RealInput() {^shoulder_angle}
  "Measured pendulum (elbow) angle, 0 hanging down, on the fast clock"
  elbow_angle = Dyad.RealInput() {^elbow_angle}
  "Control command, on the MPC's clock"
  u = Dyad.RealOutput() {^u}
  "acados status of the last MPC solve: 0 success, 2 maximum iterations, 4 QP failure, ..."
  exitflag = Dyad.RealOutput() {^exitflag}
  estimator_shoulder = DiscreteComponents.AlphaBetaGammaFilter(final alpha = velocity_alpha) {^estimator_shoulder}
  estimator_elbow = DiscreteComponents.AlphaBetaGammaFilter(final alpha = velocity_alpha) {^estimator_elbow}
  latest_shoulder_angle = DiscreteComponents.Latest() {^latest_shoulder_angle}
  latest_elbow_angle = DiscreteComponents.Latest() {^latest_elbow_angle}
  latest_shoulder_rate = DiscreteComponents.Latest() {^latest_shoulder_rate}
  latest_elbow_rate = DiscreteComponents.Latest() {^latest_elbow_rate}
  anglenormalization = QuanserComponents.AngleNormalization() {^anglenormalization}
  mpc = MPCComponents.ACADOSMPC(final dynamics = dynamics, final state_variables = FURUTA_MPC_STATES, final outputs = FURUTA_MPC_OUTPUTS, final Ts = Ts, final Np = Np, final time_steps = time_steps, final Q1 = furuta_mpc_weight(Q1, energy_weight), final Q2 = Q2, final umin = [-umax], final umax = [umax], final constrained = FURUTA_MPC_CONSTRAINED, final constrained_min = [-arm_limit, -velocity_limit_shoulder, -velocity_limit_elbow], final constrained_max = [arm_limit, velocity_limit_shoulder, velocity_limit_elbow], final soft_weight = soft_weight, final terminal_lqr_cost = true, final operating_point = [0, pi, 0, 0], final nlp_solver = nlp_solver, final warm_start = warm_start, final reset_on_failure = true, integrator = MPCComponents.ACADOSIntegrator.ERK(), final integrator_stages = integrator_stages, final backend = MPCComponents.ACADOSBackend.Julia(), final max_iter = max_iter, final levenberg_marquardt = levenberg_marquardt, final qp_cond_N = qp_cond_N, final output_trajectories = output_trajectories, qp_solver = MPCComponents.ACADOSQPSolver.PartialCondensingHPIPM(), penalize_increments = false) {^mpc}
  zero = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(final k = 0) {^zero}
  upright = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(final k = pi) {^upright}
  level = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(final k = 1) {^level}
  """
  Prediction model of the MPC: a `ContinuousDynamics` built from `FurutaPredictionModel` with an
  AD Jacobian backend (see `furuta_mpc_dynamics`). The default is the `QubePendulum` with the
  identified parameters
  """
  structural parameter dynamics::Native = furuta_mpc_dynamics()
  "Sample time of the MPC; the first shooting interval, so it must equal the slow clock's period"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "Number of shooting intervals, the MPC's decision variables"
  structural parameter Np::Integer = 50
  "Prediction horizon [s]. The Np shooting intervals grow linearly from Ts to cover it; horizon = Np * Ts is the uniform grid"
  structural parameter horizon::Real = 0.8
  "Lengths of the Np shooting intervals, laid out over the horizon by `MPCComponents.linear_time_steps`"
  final structural parameter time_steps::Real[:] = linear_time_steps(Ts, Np, horizon)
  "Stages of the explicit Runge-Kutta integrator acados discretizes each shooting interval with. One sub-step per interval, so the longest interval of a stretched grid is integrated in one step of this scheme; see `FurutaMPCMultirate`'s documentation for what that costs"
  structural parameter integrator_stages::Integer = 2
  "Motor voltage bound of the MPC [V]. The swing-up needs 10 (clamp the command with `command_umax` instead of lowering this)"
  structural parameter umax::Real = 10.0
  "Arm angle the MPC keeps the arm within (softly, over its horizon) [rad]; inside the end stops at ±1.92. The swing-up under LQR weights violates it, see `FurutaMPC`"
  structural parameter arm_limit::Real = 1.7
  "Arm velocity the MPC keeps its predictions within [rad/s]; a soft bound that keeps the real-time iterates where the linearization is meaningful"
  structural parameter velocity_limit_shoulder::Real = 20.0
  "Pendulum velocity the MPC keeps its predictions within [rad/s]; see velocity_limit_shoulder"
  structural parameter velocity_limit_elbow::Real = 30.0
  "Stage weight on the pendulum's energy error, the swing-up term (see `FurutaMPC`)"
  structural parameter energy_weight::Real = 1e5
  "Quadratic penalty on the slacks of the soft state bounds (arm angle and velocities)"
  structural parameter soft_weight::Real = 1e3
  "NLP solver: one real-time iteration per tick (SQP_RTI, the default) or SQP to convergence"
  structural parameter nlp_solver::MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver = MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver.SQP_RTI()
  "Initial guess of the NLP at every tick (see `FurutaMPC`)"
  structural parameter warm_start::MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart = MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart.Shift()
  "Maximum SQP iterations per tick (SQP only). Structural here, since acados sizes its memory by it"
  structural parameter max_iter::Integer = 30
  "Levenberg-Marquardt regularization of the Gauss-Newton Hessian"
  structural parameter levenberg_marquardt::Real = 1.0
  "Horizon of HPIPM's partially condensed QP; -1 keeps the full horizon Np. Re-measured at Np = 50: 3 to 10 are within a few percent of each other and 1, 2 and the uncondensed horizon are worse, as at Np = 60 (see NOTES.md)"
  structural parameter qp_cond_N::Integer = 5
  "Record the MPC's predicted trajectories, KKT residuals and iteration count at every tick, what `MPCComponents.mpc_gui` shows"
  structural parameter output_trajectories::Boolean = false
  "Stage weight on the deviation from upright in the order [shoulder_angle, elbow_angle, shoulder_velocity, elbow_velocity]: `design_lqr`'s (see `FurutaMPC`)"
  structural parameter Q1::Real[4, 4] = diagonal([1000.0, 10.0, 1.0, 1.0])
  "Control weight: `design_lqr`'s"
  structural parameter Q2::Real[1, 1] = diagonal([100.0])
  "Position correction gain of both state estimators, in (0, 1); the rate and acceleration gains follow from it by Kalata's steady-state relations, so this is the one knob. Larger trusts the measurement more and filters less. Runtime-settable, a `TuningGains` field of `FurutaMPCMultirateHardware`, and the value to sweep in test/mpc_rollouts.jl -- the only place encoder quantization is simulated"
  parameter velocity_alpha::Real(min = 0, max = 1) = 0.5
relations
  connect(estimator_shoulder.y, latest_shoulder_angle.u) {^id16}
  connect(estimator_elbow.y, latest_elbow_angle.u) {^id17}
  connect(estimator_shoulder.rate, latest_shoulder_rate.u) {^id18}
  connect(estimator_elbow.rate, latest_elbow_rate.u) {^id19}
  # Wrapped after the sub-sample, not before: `mod` is memoryless so the value is the same, and
  # this leaves every `Latest` source a variable of the fast partition.
  connect(latest_elbow_angle.y, anglenormalization.u) {^id20}
  connect(latest_shoulder_angle.y, mpc.x[1]) {^id21}
  connect(anglenormalization.y, mpc.x[2]) {^id22}
  connect(latest_shoulder_rate.y, mpc.x[3]) {^id23}
  connect(latest_elbow_rate.y, mpc.x[4]) {^id24}
  # The reference is the upright state: arm centred, pendulum at π, at rest, and the energy of rest upright.
  connect(zero.y, mpc.r[1], mpc.r[3], mpc.r[4]) {^id25}
  connect(upright.y, mpc.r[2]) {^id26}
  connect(level.y, mpc.r[5]) {^id27}
  connect(mpc.u[1], u) {^id28}
  connect(mpc.exitflag, exitflag) {^id29}
  connect(elbow_angle, estimator_elbow.u) {^id30}
  connect(shoulder_angle, estimator_shoulder.u) {^id31}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/SwingupCatch.svg"}},
  "_links": {
