  }
}
end

"""
The multirate MPC closed around the physical QUBE: the counterpart of `FurutaMPCHardware`, which
is unchanged and still the single-rate program.

The encoders are read at `Ts_fast` and the state estimated there; the MPC solves at `Ts` and
`HardwareCommand` writes the amplifier once per solve.

`HardwareDiagnostics` sits on the MPC's clock and not on the sensing clock because its `dep` port
is what orders its reads after the motor write -- `exec` is latched by the write -- and a
a `Latest` block between them would be a partition boundary rather than a data dependency, so that
ordering would be lost.

The `DataLogger` is on the MPC's clock too, one row per solve in the `MPC_LOG_COLUMNS` order, so
the file is the single-rate program's file and every reader of it works unchanged. Two
`Latest` blocks bring the measured angles onto that clock; they read the same inputs at the same
reading clock as the controller's own sub-samplers, so the logged angles are exactly the ones the
MPC solved with.

Three things this costs, all easy to over-read:

  - **The fast partition is not recorded.** The log still has one row per `Ts`; the encoder
    samples and the state estimates between solves never reach the file, which is a pity since
    they are what this model exists to improve. A second `DataLogger` on the fast clock would
    need a second log slot in csrc/qube_log.c first.
  - **Two columns mean something different here.** `dt` and `time` are latched by the encoder
    read (csrc/qube_hw.c), which now runs on the fast clock: `dt` is the achieved *fast* period,
    about `Ts_fast`, sampled once per `Ts`, and `time` is the timestamp of the last fast read, up
    to `Ts_fast` before the write. `exec`, being read-to-write, is now close to the MPC's solve
    time rather than the whole loop body. Reading the log as a single-rate one misleads.
  - **`realtime = true` paces the MPC's clock only.** In a *simulated* run (an ODE solver
    stepping the program, the `mpc_gui` route) the five fast reads bunch just before each slot
    instead of spreading. Fine for inspecting predictions, wrong as a rig run -- a rig run goes
    through the compiled program and is timed by the driver.

Whether the device sustains a `Ts_fast` of 1 ms is a measurement, not a modelling question: each
encoder read is a HIL transaction of order 100 to 300 us, and the MPC solve itself takes about
0.83 ms at the median, which does not fit inside a 1 ms slot once the read is added -- the loop
absorbs that by running the next fast ticks back to back, so the cost is jitter on the sensing
clock rather than failure. The `dt` and `exec` columns measure both. `Ts_fast` is structural, so
halving the rate is one keyword.
"""
component FurutaMPCMultirateHardware
  measurement = HardwareMeasurement() {^measurement}
  control_system = FurutaMPCMultirate(final dynamics = dynamics, final Ts = Ts, final Np = Np, final horizon = horizon, final integrator_stages = integrator_stages, final umax = umax, final arm_limit = arm_limit, final nlp_solver = nlp_solver, final warm_start = warm_start, final qp_cond_N = qp_cond_N, final output_trajectories = output_trajectories, final velocity_alpha = velocity_alpha) {^control_system}
  command = HardwareCommand(final umax = command_umax) {^command}
  diagnostics = HardwareDiagnostics(final realtime = realtime) {^diagnostics}
  logger = DataLogger(final n = MPC_LOG_NCOLS, final filename = log_file, final header = MPC_LOG_HEADER) {^logger}
  log_shoulder = DiscreteComponents.Latest() {^log_shoulder}
  log_elbow = DiscreteComponents.Latest() {^log_elbow}
  fastclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(final dt = Ts_fast) {^fastclock}
  slowclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(final dt = Ts) {^slowclock}
  "Sample time of the MPC"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "Sample time of the encoder reads and the state estimators"
  structural parameter Ts_fast::Real = 0.001
  "Number of shooting intervals (see `FurutaMPCMultirate`)"
  structural parameter Np::Integer = 50
  "Prediction horizon [s]; the Np intervals grow linearly from Ts to cover it"
  structural parameter horizon::Real = 0.8
  "Stages of the MPC's explicit Runge-Kutta integrator (see `FurutaMPCMultirate`)"
  structural parameter integrator_stages::Integer = 2
  "Prediction model of the MPC: `FurutaPredictionModel` compiled by `furuta_mpc_dynamics`"
  structural parameter dynamics::Native = furuta_mpc_dynamics()
  "Motor voltage bound of the MPC [V]; the swing-up needs 10. `command_umax` clamps what reaches the amplifier"
  structural parameter umax::Real = 10.0
  "Arm angle the MPC keeps the arm within [rad], inside the end stops at ±1.92"
  structural parameter arm_limit::Real = 1.7
  "NLP solver of the MPC"
  structural parameter nlp_solver::MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver = MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver.SQP_RTI()
  "Initial guess of the MPC's NLP at every tick (see `FurutaMPC`)"
  structural parameter warm_start::MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart = MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart.Shift()
  "Horizon of HPIPM's partially condensed QP; -1 keeps the full horizon Np (see `FurutaMPCMultirate`)"
  structural parameter qp_cond_N::Integer = 5
  "File the log is written to; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter log_file::String = MPC_LOG_FILE
  "Pace the ticks in real time from inside the program, for running this model as a *simulation* against the device. Paces the MPC's clock only, see the component documentation"
  structural parameter realtime::Boolean = false
  "Record the MPC's predicted trajectories and solver residuals at every tick, for `MPCComponents.mpc_gui`"
  structural parameter output_trajectories::Boolean = false
  "Saturation applied to the command before it is written to the amplifier [V]. Runtime-settable, a `TuningGains` field"
  parameter command_umax::Dyad.Voltage = umax
  "Position correction gain of the state estimators (see `FurutaMPCMultirate`). Runtime-settable, a `TuningGains` field"
  parameter velocity_alpha::Real(min = 0, max = 1) = 0.5
relations
  connect(measurement.shoulder_angle, control_system.shoulder_angle, log_shoulder.u, fastclock.y) {^id10}
  connect(measurement.elbow_angle, control_system.elbow_angle, log_elbow.u) {^id11}
  # The MPC's clock is declared on the command net; `HardwareCommand`, `HardwareDiagnostics` and
  # the `DataLogger` all take it from here.
  connect(control_system.u, command.u, slowclock.y) {^id12}
  # The applied voltage is the diagnostics' dependency token as well as a logged column: `exec` is
  # latched by the write, so the reads have to be scheduled after it. That is why the diagnostics
  # cannot be moved to the fast clock.
  connect(command.u_applied, diagnostics.dep, logger.u[4]) {^id13}
  connect(log_shoulder.y, logger.u[2]) {^id14}
  connect(log_elbow.y, logger.u[3]) {^id15}
  connect(diagnostics.elapsed, logger.u[1]) {^id16}
  connect(diagnostics.dt, logger.u[5]) {^id17}
  connect(diagnostics.exec, logger.u[6]) {^id18}
  connect(control_system.exitflag, logger.u[7]) {^id19}
metadata {
